\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Cancellation of the two charge maps}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{For the \vocabk{spinor time map}{fdip-017Z}, let #{t} be
the chosen time axis, #{\theta_s} its coordinate reversal,
and #{h} the reversal of all other coordinates. Use the same
identified field space and sitewise charge map #{\mathcal Q}
of commuting direction sign as in that definition. With
#{\mathcal P=(\varepsilon_t\otimes\bbI_F)h^*} and
#{S_t=(\Gamma\varepsilon_t\otimes\bbI_F)\theta_s^*\mathcal Q^{-1}},
their right-to-left composite is the
\vocabk{graded total inversion}{fdip-019C}, with
##{S_t\mathcal Q\mathcal P=U_-=(\Gamma\otimes\bbI_F)b^*,
\qquad b(x)=-x.}
It is a complex-linear unitary of square #{\bbI}. The signed
coordinate action on oriented directions has sign #{(-1)^n}.
In even dimension the formula holds with the canonical #{C}.
In odd dimension it uses the stipulated anticommuting grading.}

\proof{
\p{Acting on fields, the adjacent #{\mathcal Q^{-1}\mathcal Q}
cancel. Constant spin maps commute with site pullbacks, so
the remaining value product is
#{\Gamma\varepsilon_t\varepsilon_t=\Gamma} and the site
pullback is that of #{h\theta_s=b}. Reindexing the finite
norm sum preserves it, and #{\Gamma} is a self-adjoint
involution. These two commuting linear unitaries both square
to the identity. This calculation makes no use of
#{\mathcal Q^2}.}

\p{Each labeled coordinate direction changes sign. For even
#{n} these changes occur in pairs, each pair preserving the
individual coordinate periods and contributing sign #{+1}.
For odd #{n} one sign remains. This count uses the action on
directions even when reduction modulo a small period makes a
sign change fix all vertices in that coordinate.}
}
